  - [1mStorage unavailable[0m (private browsing, disabled cookies, some [38;5;153mfile://[39m contexts) — probed at construction; falls back
    to an in-memory [38;5;153mMap[39m. The app keeps working, preferences just don't survive a reload. Never throws.
  - [1mCorrupted JSON[0m in the stored key — treated as empty, defaults apply, overwritten on the next write. A bad value must
    never break page load.
  - [1mQuota exceeded[0m on write — [38;5;153mset[39m returns [38;5;153mfalse[39m and keeps the value in memory rather than throwing into the caller.
  - [1mUnknown key[0m — [38;5;153mget[39m returns [38;5;153mundefined[39m, not an error.

  The principle: preferences are non-essential, so this module never takes the app down with it.

  [1m4. The wired preference[0m

  [38;5;153mrememberUsername[39m (boolean, default [38;5;153mfalse[39m) plus [38;5;153musername[39m (string). A checkbox goes into the login form; on submit with
  it checked, the username is stored; on page load the username and checkbox are prefilled from storage; unchecking
  clears the stored username.

  [1mThe password is never written to storage[0m — only the username. That's a firm line, not a default.

  [1m5. Testing[0m

  [38;5;153mtest/preferences.test.js[39m using [38;5;153mnode:test[39m and [38;5;153mnode:assert[39m, with fake storage objects: a working one, one that throws on
  [38;5;153msetItem[39m (quota), and one that throws on access (unavailable). [38;5;153m"test": "node --test"[39m added to [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m. No
  dependencies added.

  [1mFiles touched[0m

